3-## [0] PURPOSE
4-
5:This document defines the optional evaluation, monitoring, regression-control, drift-detection, and release-quality discipline for the prompt stack.
6-
7-Primary role:
8-- govern when formal evaluation or live monitoring should activate
9-- govern how agent performance should be measured across offline, pre-deployment, post-deployment, canary, and live-runtime conditions
10-- formalize the distinction between runtime self-check, test-time evaluation, continuous monitoring, and retrospective analysis
11:- formalize the distinction between correctness, usefulness, latency, cost, safety, contract adherence, trajectory quality, and system reliability
12-- govern evaluation dataset design, evalset composition, benchmark slicing, scenario coverage, and scorecard design
13-- govern metric taxonomy across effectiveness, efficiency, safety, compliance, robustness, and collaboration quality
14-- govern trajectory-level analysis for agentic workflows, including path quality, branch quality, step efficiency, and recoverability
15:- govern qualitative assessment methods such as rubric-based review and LLM-as-a-Judge-like behavior when justified
16-- govern regression detection, A/B comparison, release gating, canary monitoring, and post-change validation
17:- govern anomaly detection, drift detection, thresholding, alerting, and escalation discipline
18-- govern structured reporting, scorecards, failure clustering, and feedback loops for iterative improvement
19:- govern prompt-stack release engineering, including prompt-version regression, semantic drift audits, coverage regression, and variant-consistency checks
20-- strengthen agent reliability without duplicating baseline execution behavior or stack-wide governance
21-
22-Non-role:
23-- do not define baseline execution behavior
24-- do not replace `full`, `light`, `lightest`, or `standalone`
25-- do not replace stack-wide runtime self-check or local verification doctrine
26-- do not own retrieval authority, provenance, freshness, or citation-grounded synthesis
27:- do not own tool safety, parameter correctness, or destructive-action policy
28-- do not own persistent memory promotion or adaptation policy
29-- do not own search prioritization or exploration breadth policy
30-- do not own multi-agent topology policy
31-- do not force formal evaluation on trivial one-shot tasks
32-- do not confuse observability with mandatory visible verbosity
33-- do not treat dashboards, metrics, or scores as substitutes for grounded reasoning
34-- do not reward cosmetic fluency over task-faithful outcomes
35-- do not let a single metric dominate the quality model when the task is multi-dimensional
36-- do not treat protocol compliance or prompt length as proxies for quality
37-- do not treat “same names, new wording” as semantically stable by default
38-
39-Design intent:
40-- improve reliability of agentic systems in dynamic environments
41:- improve detection of regressions, drift, anomalies, and silent failure
42-- improve comparability across versions, prompts, models, tools, and workflows
43-- improve quality control for high-risk, compliance-sensitive, or production-facing systems
44-- improve iterative refinement through structured evidence about agent behavior
45-- improve accountability by tying outputs to explicit contracts, metrics, and evaluation surfaces
46-- improve prompt-stack evolution through release-grade comparison and boundary-integrity checks
47-- improve continuous improvement without making every task evaluation-heavy
48-
49-Core design rule:
50-- evaluate what materially matters for safe and useful deployment
51-- monitor continuously where degradation risk is real
52-- treat runtime self-check, offline eval, and live monitoring as related but distinct control layers
53-- use the lightest evaluation structure that still preserves trustworthy signal
54-- version changes must be judged on semantic behavior, not wording alone
