149-## [3] CORE CONCEPTS
150-
151-### 3.1 Runtime Self-Check
152:Runtime self-check is the immediate quality check performed during one execution instance before finalizing an answer or action.
153-
154-### 3.2 Evaluation
155-Evaluation is the structured assessment of agent behavior against explicit criteria, reference tasks, contracts, rubrics, or expectations.
156-
157-### 3.3 Monitoring
158-Monitoring is the continuous observation of deployed or repeatedly executed agent behavior over time.
159-
160-### 3.4 Metric
161-A metric is a measurable signal used to assess one dimension of performance, cost, quality, or safety.
162-
163-### 3.5 Eval Case
164-An eval case is a single task instance, scenario, or contract against which the system is tested.
165-
166-### 3.6 Eval Set
167-An eval set is a structured collection of eval cases intended to measure a coherent quality surface.
168-
169-### 3.7 Scorecard
170:A scorecard is a compact representation of grouped metrics, thresholds, and interpretation rules for a system or variant.
171-
172-### 3.8 Trajectory
173-A trajectory is the sequence of steps, branches, tool calls, handoffs, observations, and recovery events an agent follows.
174-
175-### 3.9 Drift
176-Drift is a time-linked degradation or shift in performance caused by changes in environment, data, user behavior, tools, prompts, or task mix.
177-
178-### 3.10 Regression
179-A regression is a measurable quality decline relative to a prior accepted baseline.
180-
181-### 3.11 Anomaly
182-An anomaly is a pattern or event whose behavior deviates materially from the expected operational range.
183-
184-### 3.12 Rubric
185-A rubric is a structured qualitative judgment framework with explicit criteria and scoring anchors.
186-
187:### 3.13 LLM-as-a-Judge
188:LLM-as-a-Judge is a bounded evaluation technique that uses a model to assess outputs or trajectories against explicit criteria when exact metrics are insufficient.
189-
190-### 3.14 Contract Adherence
191:Contract adherence is the degree to which the output or action satisfies the explicit deliverable, scope, safety, and format agreement.
192-
193-### 3.15 Coverage
194-Coverage is the degree to which the evaluation surface represents the meaningful task, risk, and behavior space.
195-
196-### 3.16 Alert Threshold
197:An alert threshold is the condition at which observed quality or stability degradation requires review, escalation, rollback, or intervention.
198-
199-### 3.17 Prompt-Version Regression
200-Prompt-version regression is a behavior decline caused by a prompt rewrite, variant rewrite, overlay change, or cross-document semantic shift.
201-
202-### 3.18 Semantic Drift
203:Semantic drift is a meaning change in a load-bearing rule, contract, or boundary caused by wording changes, rewrites, compression, or misplaced duplication.
204-
205-### 3.19 Coverage Regression
206:Coverage regression is the loss of ownership, activation logic, or meaningful support for a previously covered design pattern or task slice.
207-
208-### 3.20 Variant Consistency
209-Variant consistency is the preservation of aligned meaning across `full`, `light`, `lightest`, and `standalone` even when wording depth differs.
210-
211-### 3.21 Stagnation
212-Stagnation is repeated execution where steps or cost increase without meaningful gain in validated progress, coverage, or decision quality.
213-
