560-## [9] SCORING MODELS
561-
562-Scoring should fit the task.
563-
564:### 9.1 Exact-match or deterministic scoring
565-Use when:
566-- output correctness can be checked rigidly
567-- schemas, exact fields, or strict outputs matter
568:- the task is deterministic enough for strong automated comparison
569-
570-### 9.2 Rule-based rubric scoring
571-Use when:
572-- exact-match is too brittle
573-- several criteria matter simultaneously
574-- human-readable quality dimensions can be formalized
575-
576-### 9.3 Reference-guided scoring
577-Use when:
578:- a reference answer, plan, contract, or action trace exists
579-- similarity must be judged with explicit tolerances
580-
581-### 9.4 Comparative scoring
582-Use when:
583-- one variant is being compared against another
584-- A/B or pre/post comparison is more meaningful than absolute score alone
585-
586-### 9.5 Mixed scoring
587-Use when:
588-- no single score type captures the risk or quality surface
589-- exact metrics and qualitative review both matter
590-
591-Scoring rule:
592-- the scoring model must fit the artifact class and operational decision
593-- do not force exact-match scoring on inherently open-ended but contract-bound outputs
594-
595----
596-
597-## [10] RUBRIC DESIGN
598-
599-Rubrics should be explicit, bounded, and operationally useful.
600-
601-A good rubric should define:
602-- criteria
603-- score scale
604-- score anchors
605:- pass/fail threshold if applicable
606-- critical failure conditions
607-- weighting if criteria are not equally important
608-- tie-breaking or override rules if needed
609-
610-Common rubric dimensions:
611-- task completion
612-- requirement adherence
613-- correctness
614-- evidence alignment
615-- safety
616-- scope discipline
617-- readability
618-- decision usefulness
619-- trajectory efficiency
620-- recovery quality
621:- ownership-boundary integrity
622-- variant consistency when comparing prompt layers
623-
624-Rubric rules:
625-- criteria should be legible enough for repeatable judgment
626-- critical failures should override average quality where warranted
627:- rubric language should be specific enough to reduce judge drift
628-- if a rubric cannot be applied consistently, simplify it
629-
630-Rubric anti-patterns:
631-- vague “good/bad” scales with no anchors
632-- too many criteria for consistent use
633-- overlapping criteria that double-count the same failure
634-- hidden weighting
635-- penalizing safe propose-only behavior when propose-only was contractually correct
636-
637----
638-
639-## [11] LLM-AS-A-JUDGE DISCIPLINE
640-
641:Use LLM-as-a-Judge-like behavior only when it materially improves evaluation quality.
642-
643-Use when:
644-- the output is open-ended but contract-bound
645-- qualitative dimensions such as helpfulness or reasoning quality matter
646-- exact-match metrics would be misleading
647-- rubric-based qualitative comparison is needed
648-- trajectory review requires nuanced interpretation
649-- prompt-version comparison involves subtle semantic differences that rigid scoring would miss
650-
651-Do not use when:
652:- exact deterministic scoring is clearly sufficient
653-- the rubric is too vague to constrain the judge
654-- judge cost clearly exceeds evaluation value
655-- the judge would merely decorate an already adequate metric pipeline
656-
657-Judge rules:
658-- use explicit criteria
659-- preserve rubric anchors
660-- separate factual scoring from stylistic preference when possible
661-- prefer comparative judging when absolute scales are unstable
662-- calibrate with known-good and known-bad examples when possible
663-- never treat one judge score as unquestionable truth
664-
665-Judge anti-patterns:
666-- using the judge without explicit rubric criteria
667:- using the judge to replace obvious deterministic checks
668-- hiding judge uncertainty
669-- allowing stylistic preference to dominate contract adherence
670-- using the judge as the only signal for high-stakes release decisions
