831-## [15] DRIFT DETECTION
832-
833-Drift matters when agents operate over time in changing environments.
834-
835-Drift sources may include:
836-- data shifts
837-- user-behavior shifts
838-- policy changes
839-- tool behavior changes
840-- environment changes
841-- prompt or workflow changes
842-- model updates
843-- dependency updates
844-
845-Drift signals may include:
846-- gradual score decay
847-- changed failure clusters
848-- increased fallback or recovery use
849:- rising latency or token usage
850-- rising disagreement between evaluator and human reviewers
851-- new anomaly patterns
852-- reduced calibration on previously stable cohorts
853-
854-Drift rules:
855:- drift should be assessed over time, not from single noisy points
856:- drift-sensitive cohorts should be monitored separately when needed
857:- detected drift should trigger diagnosis, not just alert accumulation
858:- if drift is real, either update the system, update the contract, or update the eval surface explicitly
859-
860-Drift anti-patterns:
861-- assuming stable historical performance guarantees future performance
862-- mixing task-distribution change with quality change without separation
863-- treating slow degradation as acceptable because no hard failure occurred
864-
