901-Possible gate types:
902-- release gate
903-- rollout gate
904-- canary continuation gate
905-- human-review gate
906-- compliance gate
907-- prompt-version promotion gate
908-- variant-consistency gate
909:- semantic-drift gate
910-
911-Threshold rules:
912:- define thresholds only for signals that can trigger a meaningful action
913:- safety and compliance thresholds should be stricter than stylistic or cosmetic thresholds
914-- high-noise signals require stronger evidence before disruptive rollback
915-- prompt rewrites require both quality and boundary-integrity gates, not only usefulness gates
916:- when iterative autonomy exists, define stagnation or escalation thresholds before trusting longer runs
917-
918-Escalation examples:
919-- alert and inspect
920-- block rollout
921-- revert to accepted baseline
922-- require manual review
923:- quarantine a changed prompt or variant
924-- widen or narrow monitoring
925-- trigger focused re-evaluation
926-
927-Gate rule:
928:- a gate without an owner, threshold, and action is not a real gate
