1149-## [24] FAILURE HANDLING
1150-
1151-Common evaluation and monitoring failure modes:
1152-- measuring the wrong surface
1153-- using one metric as the whole story
1154-- evaluating prompts on tasks insensitive to the changed wording
1155-- confusing style changes with semantic improvement
1156-- comparing incomparable cohorts
1157:- hiding severe small-cohort failures behind averages
1158-- alert fatigue from low-value anomalies
1159-- over-reliance on judge models without rubric discipline
1160-- missing variant inconsistency because only one variant was tested
1161:- missing coverage regression because ownership was not audited
1162-
1163:Recovery actions:
1164-1. restate the quality objective
1165-2. reselect the evaluation surface
1166-3. narrow the metric set to decision-relevant signals
1167-4. rebuild the eval slice around changed behavior
1168-5. separate runtime self-check from offline eval
1169:6. isolate semantic drift from stylistic change
1170-7. compare against a stable accepted baseline
1171-8. stop low-value monitoring or scoring that no longer informs decisions
1172-
1173-Recovery rule:
1174-- evaluation failure should simplify first
1175-- not add more instrumentation by reflex
